<article class="env-lemma" id="03FS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03FS">Lemma <span data-tag="03FS">66.16.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is étale, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a surjective étale morphism $\varphi : U \to X$, where $U$ is a scheme, such that the composition $f \circ \varphi $ is étale (as a morphism of algebraic spaces), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a surjective étale morphism $\psi : V \to Y$, where $V$ is a scheme, such that the base change $V \times _ Y X \to V$ is étale (as a morphism of algebraic spaces), 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a commutative diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ U \ar[d] \ar[r] &  V \ar[d] \\  X \ar[r] &  Y }  \]
</div>
<p>
 where $U$, $V$ are schemes, the vertical arrows are étale, and the left vertical arrow is surjective such that the horizontal arrow is étale. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let us prove that (4) implies (1). Assume a diagram as in (4) given. Let $W \to X$ be an étale morphism with $W$ a scheme. Then we see that $W \times _ X U \to U$ is étale. Hence $W \times _ X U \to V$ is étale as the composition of the étale morphisms of schemes $W \times _ X U \to U$ and $U \to V$. Therefore $W \times _ X U \to Y$ is étale by Lemma <a href="/tag/03EC" data-tag="03EC">66.16.1</a> (1). Since also the projection $W \times _ X U \to W$ is surjective and étale, we conclude from Lemma <a href="/tag/03EC" data-tag="03EC">66.16.1</a> (3) that $W \to Y$ is étale. 
    <p>
      Let us prove that (1) implies (4). Assume (1). Choose a commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ U \ar[d] \ar[r] &  V \ar[d] \\  X \ar[r] &  Y }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $U \to X$ and $V \to Y$ are surjective and étale, see Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02X1" data-tag="02X1">65.11.6</a>. By assumption the morphism $U \to Y$ is étale, and hence $U \to V$ is étale by Lemma <a href="/tag/03EC" data-tag="03EC">66.16.1</a> (2). 
    <p>
      We omit the proof that (2) and (3) are also equivalent to (1). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>